DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  nneewwllooccaallee()  function  creates a new locale object, or modifies an
       existing object, returning a reference to the new or modified object as
       the function result.  Whether the call creates a new object or modifies
       an existing object is determined by the value of _b_a_s_e:

       •  If _b_a_s_e is _(_l_o_c_a_l_e___t_) _0, a new object is created.

       •  If _b_a_s_e refers to valid existing locale object (i.e., an object  re‐
          turned by a previous call to nneewwllooccaallee() or dduuppllooccaallee(3)), then that
          object is modified by the call.  If the call is successful, the con‐
          tents of _b_a_s_e are unspecified (in particular, the object referred to
          by  _b_a_s_e  may  be  freed, and a new object created).  Therefore, the
          caller should ensure that it stops using _b_a_s_e  before  the  call  to
          nneewwllooccaallee(),  and  should  subsequently refer to the modified object
          via the reference returned as the  function  result.   If  the  call
          fails, the contents of _b_a_s_e remain valid and unchanged.

       If  _b_a_s_e  is  the  special  locale  object LLCC__GGLLOOBBAALL__LLOOCCAALLEE (see dduupplloo‐‐
       ccaallee(3)), or is not _(_l_o_c_a_l_e___t_) _0 and is not a valid locale object  han‐
       dle, the behavior is undefined.

       The _c_a_t_e_g_o_r_y___m_a_s_k argument is a bit mask that specifies the locale cat‐
       egories that are to be set in a newly created locale object or modified
       in  an existing object.  The mask is constructed by a bitwise OR of the
       constants LLCC__AADDDDRREESSSS__MMAASSKK, LLCC__CCTTYYPPEE__MMAASSKK, LLCC__CCOOLLLLAATTEE__MMAASSKK, LLCC__IIDDEENNTTIIFFII‐‐
       CCAATTIIOONN__MMAASSKK, LLCC__MMEEAASSUURREEMMEENNTT__MMAASSKK,  LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS__MMAASSKK,  LLCC__MMOONNEETTAARRYY__MMAASSKK,
       LLCC__NNUUMMEERRIICC__MMAASSKK,  LLCC__NNAAMMEE__MMAASSKK,  LLCC__PPAAPPEERR__MMAASSKK,  LLCC__TTEELLEEPPHHOONNEE__MMAASSKK, and
       LLCC__TTIIMMEE__MMAASSKK.  Alternatively, the mask can be specified as LLCC__AALLLL__MMAASSKK,
       which is equivalent to ORing all of the preceding constants.

       For each category specified in _c_a_t_e_g_o_r_y___m_a_s_k, the locale data from  _l_o_‐
       _c_a_l_e  will be used in the object returned by nneewwllooccaallee().  If a new lo‐
       cale object is being created, data for all categories not specified  in
       _c_a_t_e_g_o_r_y___m_a_s_k is taken from the default ("POSIX") locale.

       The  following  preset  values of _l_o_c_a_l_e are defined for all categories
       that can be specified in _c_a_t_e_g_o_r_y___m_a_s_k:

       "POSIX"
              A minimal locale environment for C language programs.

       "C"    Equivalent to "POSIX".

       ""     An implementation‐defined native  environment  corresponding  to
              the  values  of the LLCC__** and LLAANNGG environment variables (see lloo‐‐
              ccaallee(7)).

   ffrreeeellooccaallee(())
       The ffrreeeellooccaallee() function deallocates the resources associated with _l_o_‐
       _c_o_b_j, a locale object previously returned by a call to  nneewwllooccaallee()  or
       dduuppllooccaallee(3).  If _l_o_c_o_b_j is LLCC__GGLLOOBBAALL__LLOOCCAALLEE or is not valid locale ob‐
       ject handle, the results are undefined.

       Once a locale object has been freed, the program should make no further
       use of it.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On  success,  nneewwllooccaallee() returns a handle that can be used in calls to
       dduuppllooccaallee(3), ffrreeeellooccaallee(), and other functions that  take  a  _l_o_c_a_l_e___t
       argument.   On  error, nneewwllooccaallee() returns _(_l_o_c_a_l_e___t_) _0, and sets _e_r_r_n_o
       to indicate the error.

